raight draw (you need a 4 to make A-2-3-4-5). The pot is approximately 402 chips. You are out of position and acted passively on the flop (Check-Call). The Turn card is a King, which is an overcard to the board. Your hand has some showdown value (Ace high) and equity (4 outs for a straight, 3 outs for an Ace, 3 outs for a 5), but it is not strong enough to value bet. Leading out (betting) here would likely only fold out hands you are already beating or get you called/raised by Kings or better. Checking allows you to potentially see a free River card or keep the pot manageable.\n\nReason: You act first on the Turn. With a drawing hand and marginal showdown value (Ace High), you want to keep the pot small. Checking gives you the best option to realize your equity (hitting a straight or pair on the river) without risking chips unnecessarily against a likely stronger range or aggressive opponent.\n\nAnswer: 0'}
